Pretty routine.  Taking the logarithm we get
\begin{align*}
&\lim_{x\to 1}\log\left(x^{\dfrac{x}{\sin(1-x)}}\right)
&=\lim_{x\to 1}\dfrac{x\log(x)}{\sin(1-x)}
&=\lim_{x\to 1}\dfrac{x\log(x)}{\sin(1)\cos(x)-\cos(1)\sin(x)}.
\end{align*}
Applying L'Hopital's Rule we get
\begin{align*}
&=\lim_{x\to 1}\dfrac{1+\log(x)}{-\sin(1)\sin(x)-\cos(1)\cos(x)}
&=\dfrac{1+\log(1)}{-\sin^2(1)-\cos^2(1)}
&=\dfrac{1+\log(1)}{-1(\sin(1)^2+\cos(1)^2)}
&=-1.
\end{align*}
Our limit is in the form  $e^{L}$  and since we found that  $L=-1$  we have  $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}x^{\dfrac{x}{\sin(1-x)}}=\boxed{\frac{1}{e}}$ .

Given that  $f''(x)=0$  our function must be of the form  $f(x)=c_1x+c_2$  where  $c_1,c_2\in\mathbb{R}$ .  Notice that we also have  $f(0)=19$  and  $f(1)=99$ , which means we have
\begin{align*}
&f(0)=c_1(0)+c_2\implies c_2=19
&f(1)=c_1+19\implies c_1+19=99\implies c_1=80.
\end{align*}
This means we have  $f(x)=c_1x+c_2\implies \boxed{f(x)=80x+19}$  as required.

