This is a pretty simple derivative to calculate.  Differentiating gives
\begin{align*}
&\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\sin x - \dfrac{4}{3}\sin^3 x\right)
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\sin x - \frac{d}{dx} \dfrac{4}{3}\sin^3 x
&=\cos x -  \dfrac{4}{3}\frac{d}{dx}\sin^3 x
&=\cos x - \dfrac{4}{3}\left(\frac{d}{dx}u^3\cdot\frac{d}{dx}\sin x\right)
&=\cos x - \dfrac{4}{3}(3u^2\cos x)
&=\cos x - \dfrac{4}{3}(3\sin^2 x\cos x)
&=\cos x - 4\sin^2 x\cos x
&=\cos x-2\sin x\sin 2x.
\end{align*}
We can find the value of  $\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)$  and  $\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)$  by using the sum angle identities.  Recall that
\begin{align*}
\sin(\alpha-\beta)&=\sin \alpha\cos\beta-\cos\alpha\sin\beta
\cos(\alpha-\beta)&=\cos\alpha\cos\beta+\sin\alpha\sin\beta.
\end{align*}
In this case we have  $\alpha=\frac{\pi}{4}$  and  $\beta=\frac{\pi}{6}$  which means we have
\begin{align*}
\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)&=\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)-\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{2}=\frac{\sqrt{3} - 1}{2\sqrt{2}}
\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)&=\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)+\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{2}=\frac{\sqrt{3} +1}{2\sqrt{2}}.
\end{align*}
This means we can evaluate  $\cos x-2\sin x\sin 2x$  at  $x=\frac{\pi}{12}$  to get  $$ \frac{\sqrt{3} +1}{2\sqrt{2}}-\frac{\sqrt{3} - 1}{2\sqrt{2}}=\boxed{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}} $$  which is what we wanted.

