We can expand each integral using the product rule:
\begin{align*}
\int f(x) f'(x) \,dx&=f(x)^2-\int f(x)f'(x)\,dx\implies \int f(x) f'(x) \,dx=\frac{f(x)^2}{2}+c
\int f(x)^2 f'(x) \,dx&=f(x)^3-2\int f(x)^2f'(x)\,dx\implies \int f(x)^2 f'(x)\,dx=\frac{f(x)^3}{3}+c
&\vdots
\int f(x)^n f'(x) \,dx&=f(x)^{n+1}-n\int f(x)^nf'(x)\,dx\implies \int f(x)^n f'(x)\,dx=\frac{f(x)^{n+1}}{n+1}+c.
\end{align*}
Using our new formula, we have  $ \displaystyle\int f(x)^4 f'(x) \, \mathrm{d} x=\frac{f(x)^5}{5}$ .  Notice that we also have
\begin{align*}
\frac{f(1)^3-f(0)^3}{3}&=18\implies f(1)^3-f(0)^3=54
\frac{f(1)^2-f(0)^2}{2}&=0\implies f(1)^2-f(0)^2=0.
\end{align*}
This creates a system of linear equations, and solving it gives  $f(1)=3$  and  $f(0)=-3$ .  This means our answer is  $$ \int_0^1 f(x)^4 f'(x) \, \mathrm{d} x=\left(\frac{f(x)^5}{5}\right)\biggr|_0^1=\boxed{\frac{486}{5}.} $$

A Solution by pgmath.
This is intimidating, so we can try working with small cases.

\begin{align*}
\sum_{n_{0}=0}^{2} &= 1 + 1 + 1 
&= 3
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n_{1}=0}^{2}\sum_{n_{0}=0}^{n_{1}} &= \sum_{n_{0}=0}^{0} + \sum_{n_{0}=0}^{1} + \sum_{n_{0}=0}^{2} 
												&= 1 + (1+1) + (1+1+1) 
												&= 1 + 2 + 3 
												&= 6
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n_{2}=0}^{2}\sum_{n_{1}=0}^{n_{2}}\sum_{n_{0}=0}^{n_{1}} &= \sum_{n_{1}=0}^{0}\sum_{n_{0}=0}^{n_{1}} + \sum_{n_{1}=0}^{1}\sum_{n_{0}=0}^{n_{1}} + \sum_{n_{1}=0}^{2}\sum_{n_{0}=0}^{n_{1}} 
																			&= 1 + (1+2) + (1 + 2 + 3)
																			&= 10
\end{align*}

In general, if there are  $p$  nested sigmas, their sum is the  $p+1^{th}$  triangular number. For justification, draw Pascal's Triangle. For  $1$  nested sigma, start at row 0 and look at the first three numbers in the diagonal running left  $(1 + 1 + 1 = 3)$ . For  $2$  nested sigmas, start at the last term of row 1 and look at the first three numbers in the diagonal running left  $(1 + 2 + 3 = 6)$ . In general for  $m$  nested sigmas, start at the last term of row  $m - 1$  and look at the first three numbers in the diagonal running left  $({m - 1 \choose m - 1} + {m \choose m - 1} + {m+1 \choose m - 1} = {m+2 \choose m})$ .

Since  ${m+2 \choose m}$  is the sum of  $m$  nested sigmas, we can find the answer to our problem, which contains  $61$  nested sigmas.
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n_{60}=0}^{2}\sum_{n_{59}=0}^{n_{60}}\cdots\sum_{n_{2}=0}^{n_{3}}\sum_{n_{1}=0}^{n_{2}}\sum_{n_{0}=0}^{n_{1}}1 &= {63 \choose 61}
																																			&= \frac{63 \cdot 62}{2 \cdot 1}
																																			&= 63 \cdot 31
																																			&= \fbox{1953}
\end{align*}

