solution_md:
Wolfram Alpha needs to be fired immediately.  We can use partial fractions to solve this one.  
\begin{align*}
&\int\frac{9x+4}{x^5+3x^2+x}\,dx
&=\int\frac{9x+4}{x(x^4+3x+1)}\,dx
&=\int\frac{A}{x}+\frac{Bx^3+Cx^2+Dx+E}{x^4+3x+1}\,dx
&=\int\frac{A(x^4+3x+1)+x(Bx^3+Cx^2+Dx+E)}{x^5+3x^2+x}\,dx
&=\int\frac{Ax^4+3Ax+A+Bx^4+Cx^3+Dx^2+Ex}{x^5+3x^2+x}\,dx
&=\int\frac{(A+B)x^4+Cx^3+Dx^2+(3A+E)x+A}{x^5+3x^2+x}\,dx.
\end{align*}
This makes a system of equations that we can evaluate:
\begin{align*}
A+B&=0
C&=0
D&=0
3A+E&=9
A&=4
\end{align*}
This means that  $A=4$ ,  $B=-4$ ,  $C=0$ ,  $D=0$ , and  $E=-3$ .  Plugging these all back into our original function, we get:
\begin{align*}
&=\int\frac{4}{x}+\frac{-4x^3-3}{x^4+3x+1}\,dx
&=\int\frac{4}{x}\,dx+\int\frac{-4x^3-3}{x^4+3x+1}\,dx
&=4\int\frac{1}{x}\,dx+\int \frac{-4x^3-3}{x^4+3x+1}\,dx.
\end{align*}
The left integral is easy to compute, so we can focus on the righthand integral.  It looks intimidating at first...until we notice that 
\begin{align*}
&\int \frac{-4x^3-3}{x^4+3x+1}\,dx=-\int\frac{4x^3+3}{x^4+3x+1}\,dx.
\end{align*}
If we let  $u=x^4+3x+1$  where  $du=4x^3+3\,dx$ , we can kill this integral by performing the following:
\begin{align*}
&-\int\frac{4x^3+3}{x^4+3x+1}\,dx
&=-\int\frac{1}{u}\,dx
&=-\log|u|
&=-\log|x^4+3x+1|.
\end{align*}
Combining what we have, we get
\begin{align*}
&\int\frac{9x+4}{x^5+3x^2+x}\,dx
&=4\int\frac{1}{x}\,dx+\int\frac{-4x^3-3}{x^4+3x+1}\,dx
&=4\log|x|-\log|x^4+3x+1|+C.
\end{align*}
Plugging in the values for the definite integral, we get:
\begin{align*}
&(4\log(2)-\log(16+6+1))-(4\log(1)+\log(1+3+1))
&=(\log(16)-\log(23))-(-\log(5))
&=\log\biggr(\frac{16}{23}\biggr)+\log(5)
&=\boxed{\log\biggr(\frac{80}{23}\biggr)}
\end{align*}

