solution_md:
Notice,  $5p \leq \tfrac{n^2}{5} < 5p + 1$ , so  $25p \leq n^2 < 25p + 5$ . We can go through each value of  $n \mod 25$  such that  $n^2$  is  $0$  to  $4$  modulo  $25$  and obtain
\begin{align*}
n \equiv 1,\text{ }2,\text{ }23,\text{ }24 \mod 25.
\end{align*}
In addition, notice  $n$  cannot be  $0 \mod 5$  as that would imply  $p$  is a perfect square which is impossible. Now, we consider each of these values of  $n$ .


- If  $n \equiv 1 \mod 25$ , then we let  $n = 25a + 1$ . So, 
\begin{align*}
p = \left\lfloor \dfrac{n^2}{25} \right\rfloor = 25a^2 + 2a = a\left(25a + 2\right).
\end{align*}
Then,  $a = 1$ , but that means  $p = 27$  which isn't prime. This case doesn't work.
- If  $n \equiv 2 \mod 25$ , then we let  $n = 25a + 2$ . So, 
\begin{align*}
p = \left\lfloor \dfrac{n^2}{25} \right\rfloor = 25a^2 + 4a = a\left(25a + 4\right).
\end{align*}
Then,  $a = 1$ , but that means  $p = 29$  which is prime, and works!
- If  $n \equiv 23 \mod 25$ , then we let  $n = 25a - 2$ . So, 
\begin{align*}
p = \left\lfloor \dfrac{n^2}{25} \right\rfloor = 25a^2 - 4a = a\left(25a - 4\right).
\end{align*}
Then,  $a = 1$ , but that means  $p = 21$  which isn't prime. This case doesn't work.
- If  $n \equiv 24 \mod 25$ , then we let  $n = 25a - 1$ . So, 
\begin{align*}
p = \left\lfloor \dfrac{n^2}{25} \right\rfloor = 25a^2 - 2a = a\left(25a - 2\right).
\end{align*}
Then,  $a = 1$ , but that means  $p = 23$  which is prime, and works!

Thus, the sum of the two values of  $p$  are  $23 + 29 = \boxed{52}$ .

