Assume for the sake of contradiction that there is some set  $S$  with size  $X>n-1$  that has no such proper subsets which are also comprehensive. 
\begin{claim}
Define  $f(T)$  to be the number of  $0\leq x<n$  that are covered by sums of subsets of  $T$ . Then, if we let  $S_k$  be the first  $k$  elements of  $S$ , then we should have\[f(S_k) \geq k+1\]
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
We proceed with induction. Note that  $f(S_0) = 1 \geq 0+1$  satisfies oru base case. Next, we must have
\[f(S_k)\geq f(S_{k-1})+1\]
or else we would have  $f(S_k)=f(S_{k-1})$  which would mean that removing the  $k$ th element from  $S$  would not remove any residues, a contradiction to our assumption.
\end{proof}

Thus, we will have
\[n = f(S) = f(S_n) \geq n+1\]
this is absurd! so our initial hypothesis was incorrect and we are done.

For a path from  $(0,0)$  to  $(16,16)$  change diretion an odd amount of times, then it is possible to seperate one side of  $16$  into  $n$  lengths ( $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$ ) and the other side into  $n$  lengths ( $b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_n$ ) where  $a_i, b_i > 0$ . Then, the amount of paths which change direction an odd number of times can be expressed as
\begin{align*}
2 \cdot \sum_{k = 1}^{16} \binom{16-1}{k-1}\binom{16-1}{k-1} = 2 \cdot \sum_{k = 0}^{15} \binom{15}{k}\binom{15}{15-k} = \boxed{2 \cdot \binom{30}{15}}.
\end{align*}
We multiply by two due to the orientation of the path.

