Suppose that Anne-Marie has drawn  $k$  cards. Out of the  $2^k$  subsets of the cards she has drawn, the product always can be written in the form

\[P=2^a7^b11^c13^d\]

Taking modulo  $2$  shows us that on the  $k+1$ th draw, if Anne-Marie draws the card with the same exponents modulo  $2$  as  $P$ , she will terminate the process. Hence, she has  $16-2^k$  cards that she can draw without halting.

By setting up a recurrence relation, we find that our answer is

\begin{align*}
1 &+\frac{16-1}{16} 
&+ \frac{16-1}{16} \frac{16-2}{15} 
&+ \frac{16-1}{16} \frac{16-2}{15} \frac{16-4}{14} 
&+ \frac{16-1}{16}\frac{16-2}{15} \frac{16-4}{14}  \frac{16-8}{13} = \boxed{\frac{837}{208}}
\end{align*}

