Here's a proof with Joyal's Combinatorial Species. The standard text on this theory is *Combinatorial Species and Tree-like Structures* by Bergeron, Labelle, and Leroux, but the notation in this proof is probably not standard.

Let  $A$  denote the species corresponding to  $a_n$  and let  $A(x)$  be the corresponding e.g.f. Define  $B$  and  $B(x)$  similarly. First, note that  $B = \operatorname{Perm} \circ \operatorname{Nonempty-Set}$ , so 
\[ B(x) = \frac1{1-(e^x-1)} = \frac{1}{2-e^x},\]
so it suffices to analyze  $A$ . 

To do this, note that  $A$  is isomorphic to the species of (finite) labeled directed graphs (possibly with self loops) such that each vertex has out degree  $1$ , and if  $u \to v \to w$ , then  $w$  is labeled at least  $u$ 's label. 

It's clear that such graphs cannot have cycles with more than  $2$  vertices, and thus can be decomposed as sets of cycles of length  $1$  or  $2$  of (reversed) arborescences (which are in isomorphism with rooted undirected graphs) (This is the same idea as the species proof of Cayley's theorem), which have the same restriction on their labels. Now, let  $C_1$  be the species of the aforementioned one cycles, and let  $C_2$  be the species of the aforementioned two cycles. We have two key bijections, which we will sketch proofs for:**Claim 1:**  $C_1(x) - C_2(x) = x$ .
*Proof:* Given an object in the  $C_1$  species with at least  $2$  vertices, pull down the vertex on the first level above the root with the largest label to form a  $2$ -cycle. Likewise, given an object in the  $C_2$  species, push up the smaller labeled root.
